format runs the two quoted paragraphs together on one line:

    > First quoted paragraph.Second quoted paragraph.

The blank quoted line between them is dropped, and no separator takes its
place, so a full stop is followed immediately by a capital letter with no
space. The quote now says something the author did not write.

What it should do: keep the two paragraphs apart, with a `>` line between
them.

Cause: MarkdownParser stores a blockquote as one flat string. getChildrenText
walks the blockquote's children — here two paragraph nodes — and concatenates
their rendered text with nothing between them. BlockFormatter then splits that
string on newlines and prefixes each line with "> ", so it can only ever
recover the breaks that survived the concatenation. Any structure inside a
blockquote is flattened the same way: two paragraphs, a heading above a
paragraph, or a list under a sentence.
